1667:
(a) Any artist, developer or Doraville property owner may submit an application for a public art installation in the City to the Economic Development Department. The application, which shall be on a form created by the Economic Development Department, shall provide the following information:
(1) Name and resume of the proposed artist, including photographic or video representations of artist's existing work, preferably works of public art;
(2) Artist rendering of the proposed public art installation, including specific design elements, medium, materials, and description of any work that will require local, state or federal permits.
(3) The suggested location of installation and description of why said location is appropriate for this public art installation, as well as affidavit from the property owner with consent for said installation.
(4) The desired length of time said public art installation will remain on the property (private property only);
(5) Demonstration of property rights to install proposed public art, the proposed cost of installing same, and demonstration of the existence of the funds for said installation;
(6) Proposed agreement between property owner and the City requiring property owner to erect and maintain the public art installation at the property owner's expense for the life of the installation;
(7) How the public art installation meets the theme(s) and other criteria approved by the Mayor and City Council; and
(8) A proposed and executed public art easement which shall grant the City an easement in the location of the public art and grant the City all authority to, in the City's discretion, maintain said installation and claim ownership in said installation (private property only);
(9) Agreement by the artist that the City shall have the unfettered right, in its sole discretion, without notice to, approval of, or consultation with artist, to (i) relocate the artwork to another site or location, (ii) discontinue any required maintenance, if any, of the artwork, (iii) disassemble and remove the artwork in its entirety, or (iv) alter the artwork in connection with emergency and/or public safety considerations. Artist shall acknowledge and agree: (i) that City must retain the freedoms set forth in this section with respect to the artwork for public policy, municipal planning, public safety, environmental, budgetary and related reasons, (ii) that City's retention of such freedoms is reasonable under the circumstances.
(10) Agreement by the artist that the City may make any material alteration or change to the artwork in a manner that would affect the artwork's intended character or appearance so long as the artist has been provided notice of such proposed alteration and, if requested by artist following such notice, the opportunity to provide reasonable input in an effort to accommodate any aesthetic or other concerns of artist with respect to any such proposed alteration.
(11) Subject to the foregoing limitations of paragraphs (9) and (10), agreement that the artist waives any rights he or she may have regarding the Visual Artists Rights Act of 1990, 17 USC Section 106A.
(12) Any other information as determined by the Economic Development Department and the Art Commission necessary for determination to accept or reject the installation.
(b) Upon submitting the application, the Economic Development Department shall forward the application to the Art Commission which shall consider same in an upcoming regular or special meeting taking place at least thirty (30) days after the date of submission of the application. The Economic Development Department shall place a sign next to the proposed location with the date and time of the Art Commission meeting and a picture of the proposed installation.
(c) The Art Commission shall consider the proposed installation on the basis of whether:
(1) The application is complete and proper in all respects;
(2) The proposal meets the theme(s) and requirements approved by the Mayor and City Council;
(3) The suggested location is appropriate for the proposed, or any, public art installation;
(4) The medium and materials utilized for the installations are appropriate and desirable;
(5) The proposal meets all of the criteria and requirements as set forth in this chapter, and
(6) And any other considerations the Committee finds relevant to its determination on the application.
The Art Commission shall approve or deny the public art installation proposal pursuant to the above criteria in its sole discretion. The decision of the Art Commission shall be final and not appealable.
(d) If the Art Commission approves the proposed public art installation, they shall forward the application to the Mayor and City Council, which shall review the proposed maintenance agreement and public art easement and, upon recommendation of the City Attorney, approve or disapprove the maintenance agreement and public art easement on the basis of whether they serve the purpose for maintenance and existence of the installation as approved public art.
(Ord. No. 2021-06, § 1, 1-20-21)
